ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277956 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277956 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277956 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186276007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277956 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8284499e-ae11-52db-8d44-1ea843ffae18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186277504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277991 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9529dcc8-3804-5b8d-be78-f7e1b203fd7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186275802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278075 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c67151bb-afad-5510-9e88-7cde83548476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186276501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d99b0f37-11a6-51ae-b457-b0354976d304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278422 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278422 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/cece4e226becb315d6d951a8ae782750da79209d9a67ec52a8cd4d134999e9e6.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a533e788-011b-5fa3-9d77-cd8a43ced2a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278422 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/21/21e29d99d2d5330db2d946d981e4bd0f820ce26297bf95f962cb3000f0998136.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}(4-{r}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[2{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot (18-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-18\\pi +0 \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278430 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/53/534c80a8318ebfe21ba3f21f04ae145f999d6d83c34a402294c789bb2090e014.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1daafe6b-326b-58b0-829c-25583c57b290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278430 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/7f/7f44bc35687020594b0d26a2e25b9dbd2b8d75c964a90184626afad6afc1b1e8.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1)+0 \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278436 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278436 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87215c50-3d7b-51ff-bbb4-0e6c4fa9c71c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278436 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(x(x-y)+y(y+x)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =108\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278441 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8f1bee6-220a-53d7-9c95-ba6fb2fa5c32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278441 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186476000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1045 "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186475999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475439 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "41a71fe0-482d-55e4-85d0-de94d279d70a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475463 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475463 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475463 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0de413f9-cd7a-535a-8ffa-46d5c7da302d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475498 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475498 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/re